index 42a93f8..8e47654 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -86,6 +86,25 @@ were only verified for 1.3.1 and 1.3.4.
 
 `dlssg_to_xefg_intel_is_better.log` next to the dll says which one was picked.
 
+## Diagnostics
+
+Setting the environment variable `DLSSG_TO_XEFG_DIAGNOSTICS=1` before the game starts makes the dll log where time and
+video memory go. Without it the dll does exactly what it did before. With it, the dll records two timestamp queries
+around each of its own regions in the game's command list and asks libxess_fg.dll how much memory it wants:
+
+- `XeFG memory at WxH: ...` each time a context is created: the temp buffer heap, the temp texture heap and the
+  persistent resources of XeFG itself. Only for XeFG 1.3.4. It depends on the output resolution and on the model XeFG
+  uses, and it is a lower bound: XeFG allocates a few more resources that this call does not report.
+- `<purpose>: WxH, format F (N), X MiB` for every texture the dll creates itself, the copies of the game's inputs and
+  the outputs of the generated frames.
+- `GPU time avg/max in ms: ...` about every 480 timed regions. `capture` is the copy of a real frame's inputs (once per
+  real frame), `execute pair` is XeFG for generated frame 1 (the per pair work, which includes the optical flow and the
+  CUDA layers, plus that frame), `execute frame` is XeFG for generated frames 2 and up, `output copy` is the generated
+  frame copied into the game's output.
+
+The timestamps are read back a few real frames after they were recorded, without waiting on a fence. They are for
+comparing two builds or two settings, not for anything exact.
+
 ## Build
 
 Visual Studio 2026 (v145 toolset), open `dlssg-to-xefg.sln` and build Release x64. The output lands in `bin\Release`.
